  "verbalization": "Anileridine exhibits a potential relationship with Oprm1 through a series of molecular similarities and binding interactions. The evidence suggests that Anileridine, which resembles Fenspiride and Sufentanil, binds to Oprm1 via a shared structural motif, implying a possible mechanism of action similar to that of Sufentanil. This is further supported by the fact that Anileridine also resembles Difenoxin, which binds to Oprm1, indicating a broader class of compounds that may interact with the opioid receptor. The lowest common ancestors of these compounds—Piperidines and Carbonyl Compounds—suggest a common biochemical pathway, reinforcing the likelihood of a shared mechanism of action. The integrated evidence points to a potential role for Anileridine in modulating opioid receptor activity, though the exact nature of its interaction with Oprm1 remains to be fully elucidated.",
